{
  "abstract": "Does giving a frozen activation reader more source positions improve complete factual recovery, and does asking it for several records preserve that benefit? We cross a leading 32-position window versus a union of up to four located windows with single-record versus joint-list output, using the three-layer Activation Oracle on Qwen3-8B. An exposed 48-authored/12-native development study motivates an independently committed 72-prefix evaluation with 36 paired groups, three fixed decoding draws, donor activations, identical-prompt disabled-hook controls and local text. Author annotations distinguish supported complete entity/value/unit records from contradicted and unresolved claims, and close before efficacy analysis. In the independent sample, union/single raises supported records from 0.037 to 0.157 per answer, a gain of 0.120 [0.051, 0.199], while reducing unresolved burden from 0.792 to 0.588. Contradictions increase from 0.125 to 0.171, so the predeclared empirical nonworsening criterion fails. Recovery exceeds paired donors and the exact-prompt null, but local text has stronger point estimates. Joint enumeration raises recovery to 0.208 while clean recovery falls to 0.019 and both error burdens rise substantially. The source inventory contains no arithmetic-disagreeing completed total, leaving mistake recovery untested. Sixty of 72 sources reach the fixed reasoning cap; families and domains are reused, and judgments are author-only. Complete native-event releases reconstruct both audit stages and all estimates under Python 3.10 and 3.12. The contribution is a controlled separation of access and enumeration costs, with independent evidence for useful activation-dependent recovery and an explicit contradiction tradeoff, rather than a general faithful-reader solution.",
